CATEGORY 4 – SAP GRC ACCESS CONTROL 
Chapter 6 – MSMP & BRF+ (Workflow Engine Deep Dive) 
 
Q68. What is MSMP? 
Interviewer's Objective 
The interviewer wants to determine whether you understand the workflow 
architecture behind SAP GRC rather than simply approving requests. 
 
Executive Answer 
MSMP (Multi-Step Multi-Process Workflow) is the workflow engine used by SAP 
GRC Access Control to automate approval processes. 
It determines: 
• Who approves the request  
• In what order approvals occur  
• Parallel or sequential approvals  
• Escalation rules  
• Notifications  
• Workflow completion  
MSMP separates workflow logic from business logic, making workflows 
configurable rather than hardcoded. 
 
Business Benefits 
MSMP provides: 
• Standardized approvals  
• Workflow automation  
• Audit trail  
• Flexible routing  
• Escalation management  
• Compliance  

 
Q69. Explain the MSMP Architecture. 
Executive Answer 
The MSMP workflow consists of several logical components. 
Access Request 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Process ID 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Workflow Path 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Stages 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Agents 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Notifications 
        │ 
        ▼ 
Provisioning 
 
Components 
Component Purpose 
Process Defines workflow type 
Path Determines routing 
Stage Individual approval step 
Agent Approver 
Action Approve / Reject / Forward 
Notification Email alerts 
 

Q70. What is a Process ID? 
Executive Answer 
A Process ID uniquely identifies a workflow. 
Examples: 
• New User Request  
• Role Assignment  
• Firefighter Assignment  
• Role Modification  
• User Deletion  
Each Process ID has its own: 
• Workflow  
• Approval stages  
• BRF+ rules  
• Notifications  
 
Example 
Process ID 
 
SAP_GRAC_ACCESS_REQUEST 
 
↓ 
 
Manager Approval 
 
↓ 
 
Role Owner 
 
↓ 
 
Security 
 
↓ 

 
Provision 
 
Q71. What is a Stage? 
Executive Answer 
A Stage is one approval step within an MSMP workflow. 
Examples 
Stage 1 
Manager Approval 
↓ 
Stage 2 
Role Owner 
↓ 
Stage 3 
Security Review 
↓ 
Stage 4 
Provisioning 
Each stage must complete before the workflow moves to the next stage unless 
parallel approval is configured. 
 
Q72. What is a Path? 
Executive Answer 
A Path defines the sequence of stages. 
Example 
Path A 
 
Manager 
 
↓ 

 
Security 
 
↓ 
 
Provision 
 
----------------------- 
 
Path B 
 
Manager 
 
↓ 
 
Risk Owner 
 
↓ 
 
Compliance 
 
↓ 
 
Provision 
BRF+ determines which path should be selected. 
 
Q73. What is an Agent? 
Executive Answer 
An Agent is the person or group responsible for approving a workflow stage. 
Examples: 
• Manager  
• Role Owner  
• Security Administrator  
• Compliance Officer  
• Firefighter Owner  

Agents can be determined: 
• Statically  
• Dynamically using BRF+  
• From HR organizational data  
• Through organizational rules  
 
Q74. Explain BRF+. 
This is one of the most frequently asked technical questions. 
 
Executive Answer 
BRF+ (Business Rule Framework Plus) is SAP's rule engine used to make workflow 
decisions. 
MSMP controls how the workflow runs. 
BRF+ decides where it goes. 
BRF+ determines: 
• Approvers  
• Paths  
• Notifications  
• Escalations  
• Conditional routing  
 
Example 
IF Company Code = 1000 
 
↓ 
 
Finance Manager 
 
ELSE 
 
Finance Director 

No ABAP coding is required. 
 
Q75. Difference Between MSMP and BRF+ 
MSMP BRF+ 
Workflow engine Rule engine 
Controls workflow Controls decisions 
Stages Routing logic 
Approval sequence Business rules 
Notifications Conditions 
 
Interview Tip 
A simple way to explain it is: 
MSMP is the road. BRF+ is the GPS deciding which road to take. 
 
Q76. Explain Parallel vs Sequential Approval. 
Sequential Approval 
Approvers act one after another. 
Manager 
 
↓ 
 
Role Owner 
 
↓ 
 
Security 
 
↓ 
 
Provision 
 

Parallel Approval 
Multiple approvers receive the request simultaneously. 
Manager 
 
─────┐ 
 
Role Owner 
 
─────┘ 
 
↓ 
 
Provision 
 
When to Use 
Sequential: 
• High-risk requests  
• Financial access  
• Production access  
Parallel: 
• Low-risk requests  
• Faster turnaround  
• Independent approvals  
 
Q77. How does MSMP determine Approvers? 
Executive Answer 
Approvers can be determined using: 
• Manager hierarchy  
• Role Owner  
• HR Organizational Structure  
• BRF+ Decision Tables  

• Custom Rules  
• Compliance Owners  
Dynamic determination improves scalability and reduces manual maintenance. 
 
Q78. Explain Escalation in MSMP. 
 
Executive Answer 
Escalation ensures workflows continue if an approver does not respond. 
Example 
Manager 
↓ 
No response for 48 hours 
↓ 
Reminder 
↓ 
Escalation to Director 
↓ 
Approval 
Escalations prevent workflow bottlenecks. 
 
Q79. Explain Notifications. 
 
Notifications inform users about workflow events. 
Examples: 
• New Request  
• Approval Required  
• Request Approved  
• Request Rejected  

• Firefighter Review  
• Provisioning Completed  
Notifications improve visibility and reduce delays. 
 
Q80. How do you troubleshoot a stuck MSMP Workflow? 
This is one of the most common production support questions. 
 
Executive Answer 
I follow a structured troubleshooting methodology. 
Step 1 
Check workflow status. 
 
Step 2 
Identify current stage. 
 
Step 3 
Verify BRF+ rule execution. 
 
Step 4 
Confirm agent determination. 
 
Step 5 
Review escalation rules. 
 
Step 6 
Check notification delivery. 
 
Step 7 

Validate connector and provisioning status. 
 
Step 8 
Review application logs. 
 
Step 9 
Coordinate with Basis if RFC or system issues exist. 
 
Step 10 
Test the corrected workflow in QA before implementing changes in Production. 
 
Q81. Common MSMP Issues 
Issue Possible Cause 
Request stuck Missing approver 
Wrong approver BRF+ rule error 
Duplicate approvals Incorrect path design 
No notification Mail configuration issue 
Provisioning failed Connector/RFC problem 
Workflow loops Incorrect routing logic 
 
Q82. Real Interview Scenario 
Interviewer 
"A Production Access Request is waiting for approval. The manager has left the 
company. What would you do?" 
Executive Answer 
I would first confirm where the request is waiting within the MSMP workflow and 
verify why the approval has not progressed. I would then review how the approver is 
determined—through HR hierarchy, Role Owner assignment, or BRF+ rules. 

If the manager has left the organization, I would work with HR or the business to 
identify the officially designated replacement or delegate rather than manually 
bypassing the workflow. If delegation or substitution is already configured, I would 
verify that it is functioning correctly. After resolving the immediate request, I would 
update the organizational data or BRF+ rules to ensure future requests are routed 
to the correct approver. 
This preserves governance while avoiding recurring workflow failures. 
 
Q83. Real Manufacturing Scenario 
Interviewer 
"During an S/4HANA go-live weekend, hundreds of access requests are delayed 
because the approval workflow is too long. Business leaders ask you to remove 
several approval steps temporarily. What would you recommend?" 
Executive Answer 
I would avoid removing governance controls without assessing the associated risk. 
My approach would be: 
1. Categorize requests by risk level.  
2. Fast-track low-risk requests through an approved simplified workflow.  
3. Retain full approvals for high-risk or production-critical access.  
4. Enable temporary parallel approvals where appropriate to reduce 
turnaround time.  
5. Ensure ARA continues to run before provisioning.  
6. Monitor workflow volumes throughout the go-live period and restore the 
standard workflow once hypercare is complete.  
This approach maintains compliance while supporting business continuity during a 
critical deployment. 
 
Senior Consultant Tips 
When discussing MSMP and BRF+: 
• Emphasize workflow governance, not just configuration.  
• Explain how BRF+ enables dynamic routing without custom ABAP.  

• Highlight business continuity through escalation and delegation.  
• Demonstrate a structured troubleshooting methodology for workflow 
issues.  
• Connect your answers to S/4HANA transformation and manufacturing go-
live scenarios, where workflow efficiency is critical.  
 
